- Hidden mixed-dimensional reference, not yet in the local catalog: Bohrdt et al., Nature Physics 18, 651 (2022), [arXiv:2108.04118](https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04118). Mixed-dimensional means two-dimensional layers with interlayer spin exchange and suppressed interlayer hopping. Their DMRG is mainly about doped pairing. The half-filled reading used here is the author's: interlayer exchange dominant gives an interlayer-singlet product; intralayer coupling dominant gives decoupled-layer antiferromagnetism, close to a single-layer Hubbard model. Do not treat that reading as a figure already plotted under the name SMG.
- Author-side context, not solver input: interlayer spin exchange on a half-filled square-lattice bilayer. Weak coupling has an exciton instability that folds the zone and depends on nesting. Strong coupling is a symmetry-preserving gapped state. At half filling that state is not superconducting. Doping moves the system off the filling where the symmetric gap is allowed, and interlayer singlet superconductivity can develop, with a strong-coupling peak near quarter filling. Changing a second hopping relative to the nearest-neighbor hopping is the intended way to spoil the Fermi-surface shape; that scan is not a figure in either paper.
